uh no the deadline for the Prime  Minister issues sunac to decide whether  to block Scotland's gender laws will  arrive this Wednesday the bill would  allow trans people to obtain a gender  recognition certificate without the need  for medical diagnosis  joined Now by campaigner Ellie gomasol  who is president of the national Union  of students in Scotland hello to you  it's good to see you thanks very much  indeed for joining us on the program  this morning subject very close to your  heart tell us about your journey through  transitioning  well I came out as trans when I was 18  and that was about five years ago now  I'm 23 now I had a really positive  experience  um all of my friends and family were  really really supportive  um I have to admit though I often joke  that the hardest thing about being trans  is the admin and I think there's a lot  of Truth to that it's been one of the  the biggest barriers one of the biggest  hurdles uh for me  um I've been able to update things like  my passport my driving license  Etc to to reflect my my name uh and my  my gender and the one thing that I've  not been able to do is update my birth  certificate and and that's because that  requires one of these gender recognition  certificates in order to do that that's  all the gender recognition certificate  does actually it allows you to update  your birth certificate and that means  that if you're getting married and you  can make sure that the marriage is being  recorded with you and you're correct  gender it means also that if you pass  away and that you can have dignity in  death by ensuring that the death  certificate is recorded correctly now  I've been not I've not been able to to  get that certificate and because I've  been on the waiting list for the NHS  gender identity clinics for that medical  diagnosis uh for coming up for five  years now I've not had an initial  appointment yet I'm still waiting I  don't know when that's going to be and  that means that under the current  process if I wanted to get that gender  recognition certificate to update my  birth certificate I would have to pay  for a private psychiatric assessment I  mean that costs hundreds of pounds on  top of all of the other costs related to  Gathering all of the the you know other  doc documents that they ask you to  submit to this sort of panel of  so-called experts who will never  actually meet you to determine whether  you're man enough or woman enough to  update your birth certificate and so  that's been a real barrier to me and so  I was really really excited and really  happy that the the the bell in Scotland  that you know we've been campaigning on  for so so long uh finally passed back in  December to make that process just a  little bit easier  can you when you are 16 years old  know that  this sort of decision that will change  your life forever is the right one  I think you absolutely can I mean I  certainly did I didn't come out until I  was 18. uh but I think I always knew uh  that I was trans and actually there's so  many uh young trans kids uh whose  transitioned you know some of them at  Primary School they've lived you know  the vast majority of their lives by the  time that they're 16 uh and that their  new gender  um and I think what's really important  when we're talking about this particular  bill is that all it is is about updating  your birth certificate that's all that  the gender recognition certificate does  we're not talking about Medical  Treatments here we're not talking about  anything else we're talking about a  piece of paper which you know worst case  scenario if uh you know years later down  the line actually realize you know what  that wasn't right for me and you know  that that's not gonna change your entire  life uh if you need to change that back  so we are just talking about a bit of  paper here and I think when we're  talking about 16 17 year olds in  particular I mean right now in Scotland  16 year olds can vote in Scottish  elections can get married and you can  have a child you can join the Army I  think you know a lot of those last  couple of ones you know that that's a  much bigger impact on your life uh than  you know updating the the gender that's  marked on your birth certificate so I  think that not only is 16 young enough I  think that actually in the cases where  you know kids have been living in their  current gender for for almost their  entire lives at some point the majority  of their their childhoods actually I  think that the bill doesn't go far  enough and there does need to be  provision in the future for for younger  people to be able to with the support  you're in the right guidance and advice  be able to update uh you know their  birth certificates to get that gender  recognition certificate as well because  if you're a trans young person who's  been you know at school a lot of maybe  the kids are at high school if you  transition to primary school don't even  know that you're trans and then maybe  you're going to college or university  for the first time or getting your first  job you don't have any other forms of ID  and having to present that that birth  certificate that has the wrong name and  gender on it I think can be really  difficult and stigmatizing for for that  young person do you feel that given that  you know you're an expert on this matter  do you feel that you could put a number  on that age limit  well I I think that uh you know it's  something that that needs to be looked  at in more detail  um I think you know I I couldn't say  because you know I wasn't a kid myself  when I did uh come out exactly what that  age should be but I think that that you  know a lot of uh campaigners are talking  about you know sort of uh you know 10 uh  that sort of age  um but you know I think that the point  is is that we've not looked into this  enough uh about you know kids who are  under 16 but we have talked for a very  very long time about 16 17 year olds in  Scotland I mean this discussion has been  going on for about six seven years now  uh since the the leaders of all of the  the parties in the Scottish Parliament  including the conservative party uh  first committed to these reforms so I  think that that we've had so many years  of consultation and talking about this  issue that that I think that we have you  know reached a solid conclusion here on  16 17 Euro worlds and we need to have  more discussions about younger people  than that to make sure that those those  people in the future are able to access  gender recognition certificates as well  as you know zakias Dharma does not agree  with you he thinks that 16 is too young  for a young person to decide on  paperwork when it relates to their  gender and we know that the British  government also has concerns about the  bureaucracy of having different roles in  Scotland than for the rest of the union  and could in fact as a result block this  bill what would you say to the Prime  Minister this morning  or I would say look at the Belt look at  what it actually does look at where it  actually changes uh the the law and I  think when we've been talking about this  bill and in particular uh the lines that  have been coming from the UK government  all relate uh to the equality act the  equality act 2010  um which is the the law that you know  puts into place uh you know  anti-discrimination uh measures Etc  um it's the law that currently means  that you know trans people are able to  to access spaces for instance that match  the gender that we are rather than the  sex that we were assigned at Birth  um and I think that that you know all of  these conversations don't actually  relate to the really simple process of  updating your birth certificate which is  what this uh what this bill actually  does I I think that that you know we've  ended up getting into all of these  conversations about different Provisions  that the bill doesn't actually change so  what I'd say to both the Prime Minister  and to to kierstama is look at what the  bill actually does and listen to the  people who have been looking at this for  years particularly to to cure starmer I  mean you know the Scottish labor party  fully supported these reforms Mark  Drakeford the first minister of Wales  has also said that you know if Wales had  the same Powers as Scotland then this  would be something that the Welsh  government should be looking at as well  and you know in the conservatives  Theresa May has supported these reforms  and a number of Scottish conservative  msps also voted for the bill back in  December so I say listen to the people  who have been working on this for years  look at the legislation look at what it  does and doesn't do and I think that  once you've done that the facts are  really simple yeah I mean I'm not sure  that it's completely correct to say that  it was fully supported by the labor  party in Scotland it wasn't fully  supported by the SNP as well really if I  could quote Joanna Cherry to you from  the SMP not at all happy about women's  safety and what may happen as a result  of this bill  she says the bill will allow any man to  declare himself a woman and to gain the  legal status of woman for all or most  purposes after three months of living as  a woman what it means to live as a woman  is also left undefined and some using  that as an example of  previously being recognized as a man now  being recognized as a woman and being  potentially in a woman's jail  well I think there's a lot of things to  to unpack here first of all I think that  it's right that we don't have a  state-defined definition  um of what Womanhood means I think that  would be really dangerous not just for  Trans people but for for women across  the country  um if there was a strict set of  guidelines as to what it meant to to be  a woman that would be really worrying  when it comes to things like jails again  that's something that's covered under  the equality act 2010 this bill doesn't  change that and if you actually look at  again the equality act 2010 if you look  at section uh sorry paragraph 28 of the  equality act there's a specific section  in there which talks about when there is  a legal need to still be able to  discriminate so that means that when  we're talking about things like jail for  instance you know if there was a genuine  threat uh for example for someone going  to say a women's prison  um then under the equality act that can  still be broken and that's regardless of  whether you've got a gender recognition  certificate or not it doesn't come into  it so I I think that when we're then  talking about uh you know whether or not  a man would be able to to fraudulently  claim that he was a woman and get a  gender recognition certificate I'd ask  well why on Earth would a predatory man  do that why would they go through that  process of getting a piece of paper that  allows them to update their birth  certificate that doesn't change their  access to things like Single Sex spaces  for instance all it does is it means  that you know the birth certificate now  says that they're a woman if they were  to get married they'd be recorded as a  bride and their death certificate would  say that they were a woman I don't see  any reason why any predatory man would  see that as as a bonus or a way to to  access uh you know being being a  predator okay  um eloquently Paul Ellie it's good to  talk to you thanks for taking the time  this morning I appreciate it thank you

